bc on OS-9 -- what was changed=============================GNU bc 1.01, comp.sources.reviewed volume 1, 25 November 1991, by Philip A.Nelson.  ORIG/ holds the release as posted and is what everything below ismeasured against.1.  THE LEXER DID NOT ACCEPT CR, AND THAT MADE IT UNUSABLE HERE.    bc's scanner is flex output.  Its newline rule is "\n", and anything    that matches no rule reaches a catch-all that reports        illegal character:    Every text file on this disk ends in CR.  So a bc script written with    the tools on this disk was refused outright, and so was anything piped    into bc -- `echo "2^200" | bc' produced nothing but errors.    The same held one rule further down.  The quoted-newline rule is    \\\n -- a backslash then a newline -- and bc's OWN maths library,    libmath.b, uses backslash continuations and ships CR-terminated.  So    `bc -l' failed on libmath.b's lines 167 and 173 and then reported    "Function a not defined", because the definition it could not read was    a().    Both rules now take CR as well as LF:        scan.l   [\n\r]   and   \\[\n\r]        scan.c   the same, at the catch-all, since the generated scanner                 routes an unmatched character there    The change is in scan.l -- the real source, for anyone regenerating    with flex -- and in scan.c, which is what actually compiles.  LF input    still works: ORIG/Test/fact.b is LF-terminated and was kept as the    control, so the fix could not silently trade one terminator for the    other.2.  config.h IS WRITTEN HERE, not by `configure'.    bc's configure script decides what to define by looking for headers in    /usr/include, which tells it nothing on OS-9.  config.h says instead    what the collection's compatibility headers (SRC/COMPAT) actually    supply, and defines EINTR, which OS-9 has not got: its error names are    E_ILLFNC, E_BUSERR and the rest.  flex's YY_INPUT retries an    interrupted read by testing errno against EINTR, so it needs a value    that no OS-9 error ever has.3.  BUILD NOTES.    The recipe uses CPP2 -- GNU's preprocessor -- because Microware's cpp    bus-errors on a logical line of 512 characters or more, and the    generated scanner has them.  getopt and vsprintf come from the    collection's SRC/unixlib, and vfprintf from the shipped alib.l.    No yacc and no lex are needed: the posting ships bc.c.dist,    scan.c.dist and y.tab.h.dist, which are the generated parser, scanner    and token header.  They are committed here as bc.c, scan.c and    y.tab.h, with the two lexer rules above changed in scan.c.4.  THE MATHS LIBRARY IS A DATA FILE.    `bc -l' reads it at run time, from the path compiled into the program    (BC_MATH_FILE).  It is on the disk as LIB/libmath.b and is listed in    DOC/DEPENDS.  Without it, -l cannot define s(), c(), a(), l() or e().